{"sections":{"main":{"type":"main-page","blocks":{"heading":{"type":"text","name":"Title","settings":{"text":"<h1>{{ closest.page.title }}<\/h1>","width":"100%","max_width":"normal","alignment":"left","type_preset":"h2","font":"var(--font-primary--family)","font_size":"","line_height":"normal","letter_spacing":"normal","case":"none","wrap":"pretty","color":"","background":false,"background_color":"#00000026","corner_radius":0,"padding-block-start":0,"padding-block-end":0,"padding-inline-start":0,"padding-inline-end":0},"blocks":{}},"page-content":{"type":"page-content","settings":{},"blocks":{}}},"block_order":["heading","page-content"],"disabled":true,"settings":{"content_direction":"column","gap":32,"color_scheme":"","padding-block-start":40,"padding-block-end":80}},"celllight_hero_banner_7X4atV":{"type":"celllight-hero-banner","name":"CellLight hero banner","settings":{"eyebrow":"science-evidence","title_strong":"The clinical logic behind 660nm & 850nm wavelengths.","title_soft":"","description":"","button_label":"","button_link":"","max_width":1560,"hero_height":730,"card_radius":44,"section_padding_y":32,"title_size_desktop":62,"title_size_mobile":28,"body_size":17,"button_size":18,"page_bg":"#f3efe9","text_color":"#fff8f5","text_soft_color":"#ffc0ba","button_bg":"#f5f0e8","button_text":"#191919","gradient_1":"#f04445","gradient_2":"#ff413b","gradient_3":"#ff5665","gradient_4":"#ff8c4f"}},"benefits_showcase_dM8cJM":{"type":"benefits-showcase","blocks":{"benefit_card_3dLLHR":{"type":"benefit_card","settings":{"image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/nn1_12511341-ed70-4035-a515-79a922b62606.jpg","title":"660nm vs 850nm","lead":"<p>Why Your Red Light Face Mask Needs Both Wavelengths to Shine Simultaneously<\/p>","body":"<p>If you have been shopping for a red light face mask, you have likely noticed that most mid‑range to high‑end models include two distinct wavelengths: 660nm red light and 850nm near‑infrared (NIR) light. They are often arranged in a 1:1 pattern on the inside of the mask and turn on together every time you use it.<\/p><p><\/p><p><\/p><p><a href=\"\/pages\/660nm-vs-850nm-why-your-red-light-face-mask-needs-both-wavelengths-to-shine-simultaneously\" title=\"660nm vs 850nm: Why Your Red Light Face Mask Needs Both Wavelengths to Shine Simultaneously\"><strong>View More<\/strong><\/a><\/p>","tag_1":"","tag_2":"","tag_3":""}},"benefit_card_WeFT3f":{"type":"benefit_card","settings":{"image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/nnn2.jpg","title":"Heat Therapy vs. Red Light Therapy","lead":"<p>Why You Should Switch After the Acute Phase of a Herniated Disc<\/p>","body":"<p>In the conservative management of lumbar disc herniation (LDH), both heat therapy and red light therapy are common adjunctive treatments. Yet most people overlook a critical point:<strong>the two modalities suit different phases of the disease course.<\/strong><\/p><p><a href=\"\/pages\/heat-therapy-vs-red-light-therapy-why-you-should-switch-after-the-acute-phase-of-a-herniated-disc\" title=\"Heat Therapy vs. Red Light Therapy: Why You Should Switch After the Acute Phase of a Herniated Disc\"><strong>View More<\/strong><\/a><\/p>","tag_1":"","tag_2":"","tag_3":""}}},"block_order":["benefit_card_3dLLHR","benefit_card_WeFT3f"],"name":"Benefits Showcase","settings":{"eyebrow":"","heading":"","eyebrow_size":16,"eyebrow_weight":"500","heading_size_desktop":58,"heading_size_mobile":34,"heading_weight":"800","card_title_size_desktop":50,"card_title_size_mobile":32,"card_title_weight":"800","lead_size_desktop":22,"lead_size_mobile":16,"lead_weight":"500","body_size_desktop":17,"body_size_mobile":15,"body_weight":"500","tag_size":16,"tag_weight":"500","container_width":1240,"bg_radius":0,"card_radius":34,"padding_top":88,"padding_bottom":100,"page_bg":"#f3efe9","card_bg":"#ebe4db","text_main":"#111111","text_body":"#6f6a63","text_soft":"#b1a79a"}}},"order":["main","celllight_hero_banner_7X4atV","benefits_showcase_dM8cJM"]}